    {
      "id": "LCL-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "The integral degree-two length from finite Poitou–Tate and local lengths",
      "anchor": "inputs-local-cohomology-balance",
      "statement": "Writing A=(ℚ_ℓ/ℤ_ℓ)(−e) and T=ℤ_ℓ(e+1), length H²(G_S,T)+length H⁰(G_S,A)=length Ш¹(G_S,A)+∑_{v∣ℓ}length H⁰(D_v,A). The degree-two bound uses this identity in the direction length H²≤length Ш¹+∑ length H⁰(D_v,A).",
      "hypotheses": "Use the finite-coefficient global Poitou–Tate and local Tate duality inputs and local finiteness. The global hypothesis permits ℓ odd or L with no real places. The filtration argument presented is the e≠0 branch, including the arithmetic application e≥1. The source also proves the identity for e=0 by a separate lifting argument.",
      "definitions": [
        "Ш¹(G_S,A) is the kernel of all degree-one restrictions to the groups at S.",
        "P_r consists of locally trivial classes that admit a cocycle with values in A[ℓ^r] and local coboundary witnesses also in A[ℓ^r].",
        "Module lengths are extended natural numbers. The length formula is additive before finiteness has been inferred."
      ],
